{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "sqlalchemy-azure-sql",
  "domain": "schema-and-orm",
  "value": ["corrects-a-wrong-assumption", "not-in-training-data"],
  "correction": "Agents treat SQLAlchemy against Azure SQL Database as the same job as SQLAlchemy against PostgreSQL, when the dialect silently appends OUTPUT inserted to every INSERT into a table whose key the server generates; that clause is rejected outright on any table carrying an enabled trigger, the dialect has no trigger detection and is not going to grow one, the only switch is implicit_returning=False declared per table by hand, and the same clause is why setting fast_executemany=True on ORM inserts appears to do nothing at all.",
  "value_probe": {
    "task": "We use SQLAlchemy 2.0 with the ORM against a database in Azure. Everything works except one table.\n\nThe `orders` table has an audit trigger on it that the data team added. A plain `session.add(Order(...))` followed by `session.commit()` on that table fails, and the message says the target table of the statement cannot have any enabled triggers when the statement contains an OUTPUT clause without an INTO clause. The identical pattern against every other mapped class is fine. We are not writing any raw SQL here and nothing in our code mentions OUTPUT.\n\nSeparately, and possibly unrelated, we turned on the driver's fast execute option on the engine to speed up a nightly load that inserts a few hundred thousand rows through the ORM. It made no measurable difference at all, and we cannot see the batching we expected in the traces.\n\nWhat is causing the first problem, what is the fix, and is the second one connected?",
    "gate": "The answer identifies that the dialect itself adds the returning clause to inserts into tables whose keys the server generates, and turns that behaviour off for the affected table explicitly, rather than dropping the trigger, rewriting the insert by hand, or changing the driver.",
    "states": [
      "The clause is added by the dialect, not by the application code, on inserts into a table whose primary key value the server generates.",
      "The dialect does not detect triggers on the target table and will not do so, so the behaviour has to be turned off explicitly by the author.",
      "The switch is declared per table by hand, so every affected table has to be found and marked.",
      "The two problems have the same cause, because the same generated clause prevents the ORM inserts from being sent as one batched execution."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "SQLAlchemy does not emit a returning clause unless the application asks for one, so the clause must be coming from a trigger, from raw SQL, or from somewhere else in the application.",
      "The dialect detects an enabled trigger on the target table and adjusts the generated statement automatically, so an upgrade will resolve this.",
      "The right fix is to drop or disable the audit trigger, or to replace it with something outside the database.",
      "The fast execute option having no effect on these ORM inserts is a driver, batch size, or connection tuning problem to be solved separately from the first issue."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["read", "write", "execute"],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "connect-from-python",
    "connect-to-azure-sql",
    "entra-id-auth",
    "design-azure-sql-schema",
    "schema-migrations-safely",
    "ef-core-azure-sql",
    "prisma-azure-sql",
    "django-azure-sql",
    "build-app-on-azure-sql"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "SQLAlchemy 2.0.10 or later and a driver the dialect accepts",
      "a reachable Azure SQL database, or the local container",
      "at least one table carrying an enabled trigger"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "every mapped table that carries a trigger declares implicit_returning=False",
      "no explicit returning() call targets a table that carries a trigger",
      "fast_executemany is only claimed as a speedup for inserts that carry no returning clause",
      "SQLAlchemy is pinned at 2.0.10 or later wherever fast_executemany is set",
      "columns that must hold non-ASCII text are declared Unicode rather than String",
      "no credential appears in the engine URL in source"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sa_trig (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, sku nvarchar(20) NOT NULL);\nGO\nCREATE TRIGGER dbo.trg_probe_sa_trig ON dbo.probe_sa_trig AFTER INSERT AS SET NOCOUNT ON;\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_sa_trig (sku) OUTPUT inserted.id VALUES (N'x');",
        "expect": {
          "error": 334
        },
        "why": "The whole skill, made executable. This is character for character what SQLAlchemy 2.0.52 compiles for a mapped class with a server generated key, measured on 2026-09-03 as INSERT INTO orders (sku) OUTPUT inserted.id VALUES (:sku), run against a table that carries a trigger. Microsoft Learn lists error 334 at severity 15 on the Database Engine events and errors page, which is also the correction this rewrite made: the skill previously printed Level 16."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sa_scope (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, sku nvarchar(20) NOT NULL);\nGO\nCREATE TRIGGER dbo.trg_probe_sa_scope ON dbo.probe_sa_scope AFTER INSERT AS SET NOCOUNT ON;\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_sa_scope (sku) VALUES (N'x');\nSELECT CAST(SCOPE_IDENTITY() AS int) AS new_id;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The fix, asserted rather than described. implicit_returning=False makes the dialect compile the INSERT with no OUTPUT clause and read the key back with scope_identity() instead, which is exactly this pair of statements. The same table that refuses the probe above accepts this one and still yields the generated key, so the flag costs a round trip and nothing else."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sa_into (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, sku nvarchar(20) NOT NULL);\nGO\nCREATE TRIGGER dbo.trg_probe_sa_into ON dbo.probe_sa_into AFTER INSERT AS SET NOCOUNT ON;\nGO\nDECLARE @out TABLE (id int);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_sa_into (sku) OUTPUT inserted.id INTO @out VALUES (N'x');\nSELECT id FROM @out;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "Proves the last four words of the message are load bearing: the restriction is on OUTPUT without INTO, not on OUTPUT. An agent that reads error 334 as \"this table cannot use OUTPUT\" writes off a working option. The dialect does not generate this form, which is why implicit_returning=False and not an INTO rewrite is the answer in SQLAlchemy, but a reader hand writing DML against a triggered table needs to know it exists."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sa_upd (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, sku nvarchar(20) NOT NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_sa_upd (sku) VALUES (N'x');\nGO\nCREATE TRIGGER dbo.trg_probe_sa_upd ON dbo.probe_sa_upd AFTER UPDATE AS SET NOCOUNT ON;\nGO\nUPDATE dbo.probe_sa_upd SET sku = N'y' OUTPUT inserted.id;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 334
        },
        "why": "The skill's fourth property, which implicit_returning=False does not cover. Compiled on 2026-09-03 against SQLAlchemy 2.0.52, update().returning(Audited.id) on a table declared with the flag off still emits UPDATE audited_orders SET sku=:sku OUTPUT inserted.id, and this is that statement. Microsoft Learn scopes the restriction per DML action, so an UPDATE trigger is what makes an UPDATE with OUTPUT fail."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sa_find (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, sku nvarchar(20) NOT NULL);\nGO\nCREATE TRIGGER dbo.trg_probe_sa_find ON dbo.probe_sa_find AFTER INSERT AS SET NOCOUNT ON;\nGO\nSELECT OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(parent_id) + '.' + OBJECT_NAME(parent_id) AS triggered_table\nFROM sys.triggers\nWHERE parent_class = 1 AND is_disabled = 0 AND OBJECT_NAME(parent_id) = 'probe_sa_find';",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              "dbo.probe_sa_find"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The first check in the skill's Check it worked section, run against a table that needs the flag. The dialect has no trigger reflection, so this catalog query is the only way to build the list of tables that must declare implicit_returning=False. It is filtered to one table name rather than listing every trigger in the scratch database, because the other probes here create triggers of their own and no probe should depend on the order they ran in."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE SEQUENCE dbo.probe_sa_seq;\nGO\nDECLARE @v bigint = NEXT VALUE FOR dbo.probe_sa_seq;\nSELECT CASE WHEN @v = -9223372036854775808 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS starts_at_bigint_minimum;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "SQLAlchemy compiles Sequence(\"s\") with no start to exactly this bare CREATE SEQUENCE, measured on 2026-09-03 against 2.0.52, and Microsoft Learn states that a sequence with no data type defaults to bigint and one with no START WITH defaults to the minimum value of its type. So an ORM model that declares a Sequence and forgets start=1 gets keys beginning at the bottom of the bigint range. The comparison runs in the engine and returns 1 or 0, because -9223372036854775808 is past the range a JSON number carries exactly."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sa_varchar (a varchar(50) COLLATE SQL_Latin1_General_CP1_CI_AS NULL, b nvarchar(50) NULL);\nGO\nDECLARE @t nvarchar(50) = NCHAR(0x6F22) + NCHAR(0x5B57);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_sa_varchar (a, b) VALUES (@t, @t);\nSELECT CASE WHEN a = b THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS varchar_kept_the_text FROM dbo.probe_sa_varchar;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "What String(50) costs. The dialect compiles String(50) to VARCHAR(50) and Unicode(50) to NVARCHAR(50), measured on 2026-09-03, and this writes the same two characters into both. They come back different, with no error and no warning at insert time, which is why a model that used String for user text is usually found much later. The collation is pinned on the column rather than inherited from the database so the result does not depend on how the scratch database was created, and the characters are built with NCHAR rather than written as literals so it does not depend on the client encoding either."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_sa_text (c text NULL, d ntext NULL);",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The reason the skill's third check queries the catalog rather than trusting a deployment to fail. DDL generated by a dialect that never opened a connection maps Text() to TEXT and UnicodeText() to NTEXT, and the engine takes both without complaint, so nothing between the model and production reports the problem. If this ever starts failing, the offline compilation trap stops being silent and that part of the skill needs rewriting."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
